Using 13 TeV proton-proton collision data, specifically 1.7 inverse femtobarns of luminosity collected by the LHCb detector, the differential decay rate of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ transition is investigated. To determine the shape of the rate accurately, a fit is performed on the data, incorporating theoretical predictions for the rate at certain angles, particularly at 0 degrees, as well as the rate for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{-}\gamma$ transition, serving as a reference point. The resulting fit reveals that the rate is consistent with the prediction of the standard model, particularly the Clark-Nir-Yager (CY) model, which suggests that the rate is described by the following combination of terms: the square of the difference between the amplitudes of the weak neutral and weak charged interactions, along with the square of the sum of these amplitudes. The fit parameters are determined as follows: the difference between the amplitudes is estimated as (-0.16 +/- 0.03) x 10^-3, and the product of the amplitudes' masses is calculated as (23.3 +/- 5.5) x 10^-3 GeV^3. The results are additionally used to evaluate the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}e^{+}
u_{e}$ transition rate, as well as the $B_s^0ightarrow J/\psi\phi$ transition rate, which is a mixture of same-sign and opposite-sign dilepton transitions. The evaluated rate for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}e^{+}
u_{e}$ transition is determined as (3.32 +/- 0.15 (stat.) +/- 0.24 (syst.) +/- 0.09 (theor.) ) x 10^-4 ps^-1, and the rate for the $B_s^0ightarrow J/\psi\phi$ transition is calculated as (21.0 +/- 1.0 (stat.) +/- 1.0 (syst.) +/- 0.6 (theor.) ) x 10^-4 ps^-1. The results are also used to evaluate the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}	au^{+}
u_{	au}$ transition rate, which is found to be less than 4.5 x 10^-3 times
